  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Render Lab",
    "overview": "A dark bench where one specimen — a single pi transcript — is examined under five different instruments. The page is not the product and never pretends to be: it is chrome, rules, and labels, and every colour with a point of view lives inside a frame, in the mock terminal itself.\n\nThe system is built on two registers that never mix. Outside a frame: near-black ground, hairline rules, one violet accent, and a grotesk voice that carries the argument in prose. Inside a frame: pi's real theme tokens, a monospace face, filled tinted bands, and the tool palette. A page element that borrows a tool colour is a bug, not a highlight — the frame is where colour earns its meaning.\n\nDensity is the subject, so the system refuses decoration that would tilt the comparison: no gradients, no glass, no shadow, no radius above 3px, no glyph standing in for a line of text. What is left is alignment, contrast, and the honesty of a mock labelled as a mock.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "One violet accent owns all page chrome; the tool palette exists only inside frames",
      "Hairline borders and 2px radii; depth comes from tonal steps, never shadows",
      "Monospace is material, not costume: it appears only where a terminal is being shown",
      "Every direction renders the identical 20-case transcript, so differences are design, never content",
      "Collapsed rows always reveal their hidden count; a row with nothing to open carries no affordance"
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Accent Rule",
        "body": "Page chrome carries violet (#a798d7) and nothing else. Read blue, write coral, shell gold, and the diff pair are reserved for the inside of a terminal frame; using one on the page is a leak, not a highlight.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Printed Chip Rule",
        "body": "A filled chip's text is Chip Ink (#181a20), never white. A chip is a printed label at reading size, not a glowing badge.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Label Floor Rule",
        "body": "Uppercase tracked micro-labels are 11px minimum and never run past a couple of words; anything longer is set in sentence case at body size.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Costume Rule",
        "body": "Monospace appears only where a terminal is being shown. Using it for page chrome or a technical-sounding heading is costume.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Flat-By-Default Rule",
        "body": "Surfaces are flat at every state. Depth is a tonal step plus a 1px hairline, never a shadow or a blur.",
        "section": "elevation"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Same-Transcript Rule",
        "body": "Every direction renders the identical case array. A direction that drops a case, softens an error, or shortens the 400-line read is not a direction.",
        "section": "components"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Nothing-To-Open Rule",
        "body": "A collapsed row shows +N more only when N exists, and a row with no hidden content carries no expand affordance at all.",
        "section": "components"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do keep every colour decision inside a frame tied to a tool category or a diff role.",
      "Do keep a hidden-count hint on every row that has hidden content, and drop it from rows that have none.",
      "Do express depth with the tonal steps already in the ramp — black, panel, frame, bar, nested, hover — before reaching for anything else.",
      "Do label every frame a mock, in its bar and in the page footer.",
      "Do keep the collapsed edit rule the extension's: changed lines only, context on open."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't paint page chrome with a tool tone, or a frame with the page accent.",
      "Don't add a gradient, blur, or shadow to a frame that is being compared against a real terminal.",
      "Don't round a frame or chip past 3px, or draw a border-left thicker than 1px on a row.",
      "Don't let a direction omit a case, soften an error, or trim the 400-line read.",
      "Don't let a rule, spine, or column drift out of alignment with the column it serves — alignment is the only ornament this system has."
    ]
